4
00:00:09.679 --> 00:00:10.532
Part 1.

5
00:00:10.812 --> 00:00:11.708
Problem.

6
00:00:12.958 --> 00:00:19.294
Simulate Dynamo-style replication with N replicas, write quorum W and read quorum R.

7
00:00:19.574 --> 00:00:29.003
Show that R + W N gives reads the latest acknowledged write, that weaker settings can read stale data, and how failures affect availability.

8
00:00:30.353 --> 00:00:31.228
Part 2.

9
00:00:31.508 --> 00:00:32.425
Key idea.

10
00:00:33.675 --> 00:00:43.659
When R + W N, every read set overlaps every write set in at least one replica, so the highest-versioned value seen is the latest acknowledged write.

11
00:00:43.939 --> 00:00:46.585
The read also repairs stale replicas it saw.

12
00:00:46.865 --> 00:00:54.118
With R + W at most N you get lower latency and higher availability, but stale reads become possible.

13
00:00:54.398 --> 00:00:58.174
Each operation needs enough live replicas to reach its quorum.

14
00:00:59.524 --> 00:01:00.868
Here is the diagram.

15
