00:01:55.343 --> 00:01:58.521
The write succeeds once W replicas acknowledge.

29
00:01:58.801 --> 00:02:11.601
The rest are updated later (or not, as in the demo, which leaves r0 stale). if (v == null v.version() newest.version()) rep.data.put(key, newest); repairs.add(rep.name);:

30
00:02:11.881 --> 00:02:15.700
Read repair: the read path fixes the stale replicas it touched.

31
00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:20.375
N=3 W=1 R=1, then read v1:

32
00:02:20.655 --> 00:02:25.391
The read set r0 and write set r1 don't overlap, so the read is stale.

33
00:02:25.671 --> 00:02:27.527
It's the cost of the fastest settings.

34
00:02:28.877 --> 00:02:31.117
2 of 3 down: write ok? false:

35
00:02:31.397 --> 00:02:35.130
With W=2, two failures make writes unavailable.

36
00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:37.885
That's the CAP trade-off in a single line.

