00:06:39.619 --> 00:06:41.710
What changes from the interview version.

93
00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:43.355
Here is a quick table:

94
00:06:43.635 --> 00:07:06.057
Interview, and Production (Spring Boot 4). replicas are objects in a list: separate services called over HTTP with connect or read timeouts. sequential calls: parallel fan-out on virtual threads; return at W or R successes, don't wait for the slowest replica. version counter: per-coordinator monotonic version (LWW).

95
00:07:06.337 --> 00:07:11.883
Real systems use vector clocks or hybrid logical clocks. failure = exception:

96
00:07:12.163 --> 00:07:20.953
503 ProblemDetail write quorum not met (safe to retry: the replica only accepts newer versions). validated in code:

97
00:07:21.233 --> 00:07:25.670
QuorumProperties rejects invalid N or W or R at startup.

98
00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:41.207
Two things the test exposed (see the Spring Boot overview): a 200 ms timeout fails on a cold JVM, and the N minus W calls still in flight can update a stale replica after the coordinator has answered.

99
00:07:41.487 --> 00:07:46.585
Missing pieces you should name: hinted handoff for writes to down nodes, and sloppy quorums.

100
00:07:47.935 --> 00:07:48.810
Part 8.

101
00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:50.199
Common mistakes.

102
00:07:51.449 --> 00:07:54.713
Thinking R + W N gives linearizability.

103
00:07:54.993 --> 00:08:01.009
It doesn't on its own: concurrent writes, sloppy quorums and clock-based versions can still misbehave.

104
00:08:01.289 --> 00:08:06.260
Last-write-wins with wall-clock timestamps, which silently drops concurrent updates.

105
00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:10.252
Use vector clocks plus application merge, or CRDTs.

106
00:08:10.532 --> 00:08:15.673
Forgetting that a failed quorum write may still have reached some replicas, so it may appear later.

107
00:08:15.953 --> 00:08:19.409
Clients must treat a failed write as unknown, not didn't happen.

108
00:08:20.759 --> 00:08:21.655
Part 9.

109
00:08:21.935 --> 00:08:23.279
Follow-up questions.

110
00:08:24.529 --> 00:08:25.404
Question.

111
00:08:25.684 --> 00:08:27.711
Sloppy quorum and hinted handoff?

112
00:08:27.991 --> 00:08:29.356
Thirty-second answer:

113
00:08:29.636 --> 00:08:38.383
If home replicas are down, a sloppy quorum writes to other healthy nodes, which keep a hint and hand the data back when the home replica returns.

114
00:08:38.663 --> 00:08:44.572
Availability goes up, but R + W N no longer guarantees overlap with the home replicas.

115
00:08:45.922 --> 00:08:46.797
Question.

116
00:08:47.077 --> 00:08:50.021
How do Cassandra's consistency levels map to this?

117
00:08:50.301 --> 00:08:51.666
Thirty-second answer:

118
00:08:51.946 --> 00:08:58.858
ONE, QUORUM, ALL per query, plus LOCALQUORUM (a quorum within one data centre) for multi-region setups.

119
00:08:59.138 --> 00:09:09.186
A common choice is LOCALQUORUM for both reads and writes with RF = 3, which gives strong consistency within a DC and survives one node failure.

